# The Raze base OS's Buildroot options: the `os` of raze-base. Gaia starts from Buildroot's allnoconfig, so
# EVERYTHING here is on because a line says so. Each decision is written with its reason. Kernel decisions
# are in boards/raze/kernel/; the firmware pin is in families/bcm2712/family.toml.
# F8?: the Buildroot option names below are the ones the fragment form takes; Gaia's option check (the
# coming "every requested line survives") is the test. Anything it drops is a real finding.

# -- Init and the platform ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd 258.7 (Buildroot 2026.08's version, checked in its package/systemd/systemd.mk): lemnosd's unit
# needs systemd 254+, and the Raze's units (lemnosd, lemnos-orion, Orion's comm and node) are systemd units.
BR2_INIT_SYSTEMD=y
BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD=y
# 4 KiB pages: the page size dev measured on a Raze (also in the kernel fragment).
BR2_ARM64_PAGE_SIZE_4K=y
# The Pi firmware (1.20260915, the family's pin): the boot ROM files, start4.elf and the rest, so the Pi boots.
# Its overlay set is what config.txt's dtoverlay= loads from. The Raze's device tree is one generated
# overlay (Lemnos); these two are the product's own choices (raze-device.txt):
#   bcm2712d0          the firmware applies it itself on D0 silicon (without it the kernel faults in pinctrl)
#   vc4-kms-v3d-pi5    the GPU (HeliOS wants it; dma-buf frame streams allocate from it)
#   ramoops-pi5        crash logs for the support bundle
BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_INSTALL_DTB_OVERLAYS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_DTB_OVERLAYS="bcm2712d0 vc4-kms-v3d-pi5 ramoops-pi5"

# -- The console and the way in -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A shell for Orion's logins (Orion's comm core runs a user's own shell after it logs them in, so the base
# needs /bin/sh). BusyBox's default applets; no others.
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX=y
# No sshd and no dropbear: Orion serves SSH itself (its comm core, ssh.listen), so a second server would be
# a second way in with its own keys.
# The console: a getty on the USB gadget's ACM port, ttyGS0, is systemd's serial-getty@ttyGS0.service. It is
# enabled in components/raze-base-files (a systemd unit, not a Buildroot option), because Buildroot's getty
# option is the sysvinit form. F8?: systemd's getty needs no Buildroot line.
# Console login follows the board's security setting, the one switch.
# Security OFF (the default for FRC): root logs in on this console with no
# password. It is the person's last way in when the network or Orion can't be
# reached, on a bench or a robot where physical access is already total.
# Security ON: console login must be off. How a running board switches this
# when security is turned on is not defined yet (OPEN); this fragment is the
# default.
BR2_TARGET_ENABLE_ROOT_LOGIN=y
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ROOT_PASSWD=""
# Kernel console decision (B13): OPEN, nothing masked. The kernel's console= comes from the image's own
# cmdline.txt (Gaia F10: "the image's own, then <P>.slot=..."). No file on next sets it: this fragment names no
# BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_CMDLINE_FILE, and no devices/ file has a console=. So the kernel console tty is not
# known from these files, and the getty above (ttyGS0) is not checked against it. Dev's value was
# console=serial0,115200 console=tty1 (dev families/rpi-bcm2712/gaia/slots.toml). If that is used, the kernel
# console is serial0 (dev's slot tests name it ttyAMA10 on this board), so systemd would add a second getty
# on it, serial-getty@ttyAMA10; tty1 would be a keyboard getty. Gaia has no mask form (a component's [[install]]
# copies files, [[services]] has enable only), so a mask needs Gaia's form first. Decide with the cmdline.

# -- Root filesystem ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read-only root: the slot's root is EROFS with dm-verity, which cannot be written. The writable place is
# the data partition (the slots and the state), not the root. So BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_REMOUNT_ROOTFS_RW stays OFF.

# -- Network ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd-networkd is the network manager (systemd's own; no ifupdown). OPEN: the .network file for the
# gadget's NCM interface (its address, and whether the board serves DHCP on it). ZERO 3E's usb0 lesson:
# the DHCP server must actually run. No .network file is written here.

# ---- Stripping (PR B16). Gaia's OS config starts from allnoconfig, where a bool's default does not
# apply: Buildroot's BR2_STRIP_strip (default y, depends on BR2_BINFMT_ELF) is OFF unless set here.
# It strips the target's binaries and libraries, and it also sets the kernel modules' install strip
# (linux.mk: INSTALL_MOD_STRIP=1 follows BR2_STRIP_strip). BR2_BINFMT_ELF is written out too: the
# ELF format is a choice whose default follows the MMU, so it is not left to that default.
BR2_BINFMT_ELF=y
BR2_STRIP_strip=y

# ---- Device tree overlay symbols (PR B16). The dtbs are built with symbols (dtc -@, Buildroot's DTC_FLAGS=-@),
# so an overlay can name the board's nodes. Off in allnoconfig; set here. Measured on the ZERO 3E gadget dtb:
# 55,678 -> 115,100 bytes with symbols (host dtc 1.8.1).
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DTB_OVERLAY_SUPPORT=y
